.\" ai-tools-project-registry(5) -- an operator's project registry for Agent Tools Restricted.
.\" @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@ is substituted at deploy time (install.sh install_subst
.\" and the RPM %prep), like ai-tools(1). The grammar described here is the one
.\" conf.lib.sh implements for the registry, shared by every reader.
.\" tests/unit/man.sh validates each EXAMPLES block holding entries as a file
.\" and classifies each example line whose comment opens with a state word.
.TH AI-TOOLS-PROJECT-REGISTRY 5 "" "ai-tools @AI_TOOLS_VERSION@" "Agent Tools Restricted"
.SH NAME
ai-tools-project-registry \- the project directories an operator's agent sessions may start in
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
.SH DESCRIPTION
Each operator keeps one project registry, and it decides two things for that
operator:
where a session launched by that account may start, and which agent-written
paths the ownership hand-back returns to that account.
A session starts only inside a project the file lists,
and only where no disabled or excluded line covers the directory.
.PP
The file is a launch gate and an ownership registry, not a read boundary.
Once a session is running, ordinary file permissions and the SELinux policy
decide what it reads and writes, as
.BR ai-tools (1)
states.
.PP
The file is owned by the operator at mode
.BR 0600 ,
inside a
.B 0700
directory, so no other account reads it.
.B "atr admin operators add"
creates it holding the format marker alone, so no session can start anywhere
until a project is claimed.
The
.B "atr projects"
commands of
.BR ai-tools (1)
add, disable, enable and remove entries,
and each one rewrites the whole file in its canonical form.
Edit the file by hand to review it, to comment an entry,
or to exclude a subtree of a project.
.SH FORMAT
The format marker is the first line
.B "atr admin operators add"
and
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade"
write:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# ai-tools-project-registry-format: 1.0
.EE
.RE
.PP
The marker is a comment spelled exactly so, with one space after the colon
and the version as two decimal numbers.
A reader takes the first marker in the file.
This release reads format 1.
A minor version it does not know is read, since every line is checked on its
own;
a major version other than 1 refuses the file.
.SS The file an earlier release wrote
A release before format 1.0 kept the registry in
.IR ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects ,
with allow and
.B !\&
lines and no marker.
While that file is present it stays the registry:
sessions start as before, and every
.B "atr projects"
command but
.B "atr projects list"
refuses until the file is migrated.
.PP
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade"
migrates it, unattended included.
It writes
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
in canonical form, each entry with its sign
.RB ( @
for a bare path,
.B !\&
for a
.B !\&
line) and its end-of-line comment, at the old file's owner and mode, and then
renames the old file to
.IR ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects.YYYYMMDD-N.bak .
A whole-line comment is not carried over.
The run prints the line each entry becomes first.
It names every
.B !\&
line no project encloses: in that file such a line may have disabled a
project, and in format 1.0 it is an excluded path.
Change its
.B !\&
to
.B %
to keep it as a disabled project that
.B "atr projects enable"
restores.
.SH GRAMMAR
One entry per line.
The first character of a line, after any indentation, is its sign, written
before any quote.
The commands write one space after it; a hand edit may leave none, or more.
.TP
.I "@ /home/op/project"
A project.
Sessions may start in the directory and in every directory under it.
.TP
.I "! /home/op/project/vendor"
An excluded path: no session starts in the directory or under it, and the
ownership hand-back and the claim's permission walks \(em the group, the ACL
and the secret scan \(em skip it.
The claim's label step types the whole tree, the excluded path included.
The line means that wherever it sits: inside a project, around one, or alone.
It does not take back access a claim already granted there:
a session started in the enclosing project reaches whatever the subtree's
permissions allow, and no session start checks that.
A subtree the agent is to stay out of is made owner-only: the permission walks
leave it alone, and its mode keeps the agent out whatever its label.
An excluded path may be a glob: a line carrying
.BR * ,
.B ?
or
.B [
covers each path the pattern matches whole and every directory under one, so
.RB \(oq "! /home/op/*/node_modules" \(cq
keeps sessions out of
.I /home/op/webapp/node_modules
and every directory under it.
An excluded path is not a project:
no
.B "atr projects"
command claims, disables, enables or deletes it, and none deletes the line.
.TP
.I "% /home/op/oldapp"
A disabled project: a project taken out of service.
No session starts there, and the entry, the permissions and the SELinux label
stay in place.
.B "atr projects disable"
writes it and
.B "atr projects enable"
lifts it.
A disabled line may name a project nested inside another one.
.TP
.I "@ /home/op/project   # why"
A comment, at the end of an entry.
A
.B #
after whitespace begins one.
The format marker is the one line that is a comment alone.
.TP
.I "@ \(dq/home/op/ai works\(dq"
One layer of matched double or single quotes carries a path containing a
space or a literal
.BR # .
The sign precedes the quote,
.RB \(oq "% \(dq/home/op/ai works\(dq" \(cq,
and only a comment may follow the closing quote.
.SS The canonical form
Each command that changes the file rewrites it whole, in one order:
a line sits under the nearest entry whose path encloses its own, indented two
spaces deeper, and under each entry the projects come first and the excluded
paths after them, each in byte order of the path.
A glob sits under the nearest entry its text before the first glob character
opens with, or at the top.
A comment stays on its entry, a line repeating an earlier one is written once,
and a trailing slash is dropped.
A path carrying a space,
.B #
or
.B \(aq
is written in double quotes, and one carrying
.B \(dq
in single quotes.
The readers do not read the order or the indentation, so a hand edit out of
place reads as written and takes its place on the next write.
A command that would have to rewrite a line it cannot write back, a path
carrying both quote characters, refuses and leaves the file as it was.
.PP
Blank lines are ignored.
An allow line is resolved to its real path before it is compared, so an entry
reached through a symbolic link or a trailing slash names the directory.
A line naming a directory that no longer exists does not start a session, and
.B "atr projects list"
reports it as stale.
.PP
A disabled or excluded line covers the path as written and,
where every symbolic link on the way is one the sandbox account can neither
remove, replace nor move, its real path as well:
the link and every directory on the way to it are yours or root's, and none of
those directories is writable by group or other, a sticky directory such as
.I /tmp
aside.
A link inside a claimed project fails that test, since the sandbox account
writes the project.
.SH VALIDITY
A line the grammar refuses refuses the whole file, for every reader:
no session starts under any entry in it, the hand-back does not act on any
path it names, and every
.B "atr projects"
command refuses.
The refusal,
.BR MSG-B8U2 ,
names the file, the line number, the reason, the line with any control
character shown as
.BR ? ,
and the edit that fixes it.
The reasons are:
.TP
.B unknown-form
The line starts with a character other than
.BR @ ,
.B !\&
or
.BR % ;
a bare path is one such line, since only the operator knows which sign it was
meant to carry.
.TP
.B comment-line
A line that is a comment alone, other than the format marker.
.TP
.B relative-path
The path is not absolute.
.TP
.B empty-entry
A sign with no path, or empty quotes.
.TP
.B unmatched-quote
A quote is not closed.
.TP
.B text-after-quote
Text other than a comment follows the closing quote.
.TP
.B control-character
The path carries a control character, or the file carries a NUL byte on any
line, a comment included.
.TP
.B glob-on-project
A project line carries a glob character.
Write each project by its path.
.TP
.B glob-on-disabled
A disabled line carries a glob character.
A disabled line names one project.
.TP
.B sign-conflict
Two lines name one path with different signs, the quotes and a trailing slash
set aside.
Keep the one you mean.
Lines repeating one sign and one path are not refused.
.TP
.B disabled-in-legacy
A
.B %
line in an
.I allowed-projects
file, whose grammar does not have one.
.TP
.B marker-in-legacy
A format marker in an
.I allowed-projects
file.
.TP
.B marker-missing
A
.I project-registry
file without a marker.
.TP
.B marker-repeated
A second marker.
.TP
.B marker-unsupported
A marker naming a major version other than 1.
.PP
A disabled or excluded line spelled through any other symbolic link
refuses the file the same way, under
.BR MSG-Y5N6 ,
which names the entry, the link and the real path to write in its place.
Two lines of different signs that name one directory through different
spellings, a symbolic link among them, refuse it under
.BR MSG-E3W3 .
.PP
.B "atr status"
and
.B "atr admin status"
report the file as refused, and
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade --check"
writes a
.B registry-invalid
finding for it.
.SH ENTRY STATES
Every
.B "atr projects"
command reads one state per path from the lines of the file:
.TP
.B enabled
A project line names the path.
.TP
.B disabled
A disabled line names the path.
.TP
.B excluded
An excluded line names the path.
.TP
.B inside
No line names the path, and a project encloses it.
.TP
.B ancestor
No line names the path or encloses it, and projects sit under it.
.TP
.B unregistered
None of these.
.PP
A path is reachable, so a session may start there and the hand-back acts
there, when it is
.BR enabled ,
or
.B inside
an enabled project, and no disabled, excluded or glob line covers it.
An enabled project inside a disabled one is enabled and not reachable.
A command refused over a path names the line that blocks it, and a command
that changes an entry names any other line that still blocks the path.
.PP
While a project is disabled, the hand-back does not return files written under
it, so disable a project once its sessions have ended.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/project-registry
This file, one per operator.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects
The registry an earlier release wrote, read in its place until
.B "atr admin system post-upgrade"
migrates it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/allowed-projects.YYYYMMDD-N.bak
That file as the migration read it.
.TP
.I ~/.config/ai-tools/secret-patterns
The operator's secret-name patterns, kept beside it with the same ownership.
.TP
.I /var/opt/ai-tools/sandbox-projects/
The sandbox-clone area; a clone made by
.B "atr projects clone"
is listed here like any other project.
.SH EXAMPLES
A project, an excluded path inside it, a project nested in it, and two disabled
projects, one of them nested, in canonical form:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# ai-tools-project-registry-format: 1.0
% /home/op/oldapp                       # disabled: projects enable restores it
@ /home/op/webapp                       # enabled: sessions start here
  @ /home/op/webapp/tools               # enabled, inside webapp
  % /home/op/webapp/legacy              # disabled, inside webapp
  ! /home/op/webapp/vendor              # excluded: third-party code
.EE
.RE
.PP
A path containing a space, a quoted excluded path inside it,
and a glob excluding every dependency directory one level under another
project:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
# ai-tools-project-registry-format: 1.0
@ "/home/op/ai works"                   # enabled
  ! "/home/op/ai works/build"           # excluded
@ /home/op/webapp
  ! /home/op/webapp/*/node_modules
.EE
.RE
.PP
Register a project, review the file, and disable and re-enable a project
without editing it:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
atr projects claim ~/src/webapp
atr projects list
atr projects disable ~/src/oldapp
atr projects enable ~/src/oldapp
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ai\-tools (1),
.BR ai\-tools\-admin (8),
.BR ai\-tools\-operator.conf (5),
.BR ai\-tools\-secret\-patterns (5)
.PP
The lifecycle guide covers claiming a tree in place against working in a
sandbox clone, and what each prompt grants:
